<div class="rank-val">All-time top 10</div>
</div>
<div class="rank-row">
<div class="rank-label"><strong>Open-Court Speed</strong>—</div>
<div class="rank-track"><div class="rank-fill" style="width:85%;background:#aaff00"></div></div>
<div class="rank-val">Elite for size</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>What makes Antetokounmpo's profile unusual isn't any single measurement — plenty of centers are 6'11" with long wingspans. It's the combination of that size with guard-level lateral movement and open-court speed. His long strides let him cover the full court in roughly 10 steps, about three fewer than the average NBA player. Sports scientists who have studied his mechanics point to unusually efficient hip extension and an ability to absorb and redirect landing forces almost instantly, without the brief recovery pause most players — especially ones his size — require between jumps.</p>
<div class="callout">
<div class="callout-icon">🦵</div>
<div class="callout-text"><strong>The Achilles factor:</strong> Bucks performance staff measured Antetokounmpo's Achilles tendon at roughly 13.5 inches — nearly double the length of an average person's. A longer Achilles acts like a bigger spring, storing and releasing more elastic energy on every jump and change of direction. It's one of several structural traits that help explain why a player his size moves like someone six inches shorter.</div>
</div>

