<h2>The Trade That Reshaped the Eastern Conference</h2>
<p>After 13 seasons, two MVP awards (2019, 2020), a Defensive Player of the Year award, and the 2021 NBA championship, Antetokounmpo's run with the only NBA team he'd ever played for ended this offseason. The Miami Heat acquired him from the Milwaukee Bucks in exchange for a package built around several of Miami's young rotation pieces and draft capital.</p>
<table class="data-table">
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Team</th>
<th>Key Assets Sent</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr class="highlight"><td>Miami receives</td><td>Giannis Antetokounmpo</td></tr>
<tr><td>Milwaukee receives</td><td>Tyler Herro, Kel'el Ware, Jaime Jaquez Jr., Kasparas Jakucionis, and draft picks</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>For Milwaukee, the return centers on Herro — an established scorer — alongside a group of young, developing talent and draft capital to rebuild around. For Miami, it's a direct move to pair a top-five talent with a roster that has consistently overachieved in the postseason under Erik Spoelstra. The trade instantly reshapes the athletic identity of Miami's frontcourt and gives the Heat a rim-protecting, transition-scoring force they have not had at this level in franchise history.</p>

